# Brief Jerome in his language (always on)

Jerome is ISZE pricing (Sales – Vehicle Parts). He writes like his Sent mail to Bart / Suzuki: short, factual, one topic, no flourish. He has already asked to drop internal language. A later “helpful” overview does **not** expire that.

This rule **overrides** Mode B nicknames for any overview, recap, “what you got”, Monday note, or decision ask. Sparring on a point **he named** may use his own words. Do not invent ours.

Write like his Sent mail to ITUK / Bart: full sentences, one topic, no slogans. Forbidden voice: “Monday. Five items are waiting for a name or a no.” That is not how he writes.

## His book (IN)

ISZE distributors he prices and reviews: IUK (UK pickup / D-Max, not ITUK), ITUK (UK truck), Ireland, UTI, UMI (never blended), Greece, Portugal, Nordics, CEE, Benelux, Hungary, and the rest of the ISZE GPS book.

## Not his book (OUT) unless he names them

- **MIDI** — France, Spain, Italy, Switzerland, Poland, Serbia. Spain is Tomoe / MIDI, not Jerome.
- **Spain tenders**, national portal coverage, PLACSP. Do not present as his improvement.
- Personal discount scrape (Lidl). Not ISZE work.
- Nordics API keys (already done). Never re-ask.

If a programme lane ran on an OUT topic, log it internally. Do not put it in his brief.

## How a brief is written

Lead with **what it means for his job** (list price, IUK/ITUK/UMI, Power BI cuts he already uses). Then the number. Then what the number **is**.

Every euro line needs all of:

1. **Who** — distributor in his names (Isuzu UK pickup, Isuzu Truck UK, UMI, …)
2. **What** — part in words (front brake disc, steering unit, Shell oil)
3. **The euro** — and the unit (pieces if we have them)
4. **The definition in one sentence** — billed by us / dealer sold minus we billed / still buying / no list price
5. **The period** — last 12 months, calendar 2025, …
6. **What we want from him** — call IUK, leave parked, yes/no on a real object

Never add unlike euros. If two rows use different definitions, say so in the row, not as a puzzle.

Do not rank billed sales of an account that is still buying as a lost euro, a gap, or a Monday chase. Trailing billed sales ≠ leakage.

## Forbidden in briefs to him

Do not use, even in this chat:

- File names, launchers, paths (`*.csv`, `*.cmd`, parquet, `opportunity_id`)
- Agent states (TRIAGED, REVIEW, Mode A/B, P4, G1, P8, “lane”, “productize”)
- Metaphors (“different clocks”, “name or a no”, “health call list”, “owners on euros”)
- Unexplained internal labels (Early Warning, Book B, Action Ledger, fig-key, pipeline)

If he must open something: **one** sentence, the object he already knows (“the Aftermarket command center”, “the IUK sales review”), not a path.

## Questions

A question names **the actual thing** and **who is on it**. Bad: “keep Early Warning as the sales list or treat the book as a health call list?” Good: “We already have a short list of distributors where sales look cold this cycle (UTI clutch, UMI brakes, Ireland pads). We also drafted a mail-speed flag that currently lights up 10 of 17 distributors. Should anyone use that mail-speed flag, or ignore it?”

If you cannot name the list in his words, do not ask the question. Ask nothing rather than a coded fork.

## Power BI

Talk like Power BI: figure/key, list price, billed sales, distributor. Not catalogue dimension coverage percentages unless he asked for a coverage statistic.

## Verify before send

- No OUT topic in the brief.
- No forbidden token.
- Every euro has definition + period.
- Every question names a real list or a real yes/no.
